322
00:32:15,568 --> 00:32:22,208
Chaining is the most popular technique out of everything we've seen so far in uh uh in prompt engineering.

323
00:32:22,688 --> 00:32:23,808
it's not chain of thought.

324
00:32:23,888 --> 00:32:28,848
So, chain of thought we've seen is think step by step, step one, step two, step three, do not skip any step.

325
00:32:29,008 --> 00:32:29,968
This is different.

326
00:32:30,208 --> 00:32:35,808
This is chaining complex prompt to improve performance, and this is what it looks like.

327
00:32:36,974 --> 00:32:54,528
you take a single-step prompt, such as read this customer review and write a professional response that acknowledges their concern, explains the issue, offers a resolution, and then you paste the customer review, which is I ordered a laptop, it arrived three days late, the packaging was damaged.

328
00:32:54,894 --> 00:32:56,048
Very disappointing.

329
00:32:56,208 --> 00:32:58,768
I needed that urgently for work.

330
00:32:58,928 --> 00:33:05,636
And then the output is an email that is immediately given to you by the LLM after it reads the prompt.

331
00:33:07,918 --> 00:33:20,528
so this might work, but it might be hard to control, you know, because think about it, there's multiple steps that you have listed, and everything is embedded in the same prompt.

332
00:33:21,008 --> 00:33:25,488
And if you wanted to debug step by step and know which step is weaker, you couldn't.

333
00:33:25,648 --> 00:33:27,648
You would have everything mixed together.

334
00:33:27,918 --> 00:33:40,608
So, one advantage of chaining is you know, you would you would separate the prompts so that you can debug them separately, and it will also lead to an easier manner to improve your workflow.

335
00:33:41,390 --> 00:33:49,088
let's say a first prompt is extract the key issues, identify the key concerns mentioned in this customer review, paste the customer review.

336
00:33:49,678 --> 00:34:01,284
Second prompt, using these issues, so you paste back the issues, draft an outline for a professional response that acknowledges concerns, explains possible reasons, and offer a resolution.

337
00:34:04,334 --> 00:34:09,008
So this is not, you know, prompt number three, write the full response.

338
00:34:09,088 --> 00:34:13,764
So using the outline, write the professional response.

339
00:34:14,926 --> 00:34:16,900
And then you get your final output.

340
00:34:18,190 --> 00:34:23,648
So in theory, you can tell me, oh, the second approach is better than the first one at first.

341
00:34:23,808 --> 00:34:39,488
But what you can notice is that we can actually test those three prompts separately from each other and determine if we will get the most gains out of engineering the first prompt, optimizing it, or the second one, or the third one.

342
00:34:39,648 --> 00:34:57,168
We now have three prompts that are independent from each other, and you know, maybe if the outline was better, the performance of uh the email, the email, how much it will oh the open rate will be, or the user satisfaction on the response will actually get higher, you know.

343
00:34:57,328 --> 00:35:05,892
And so chaining improves performance, but most importantly helps you control your workflow and debug it more seamlessly.

344
00:35:07,982 --> 00:35:08,388
Yes.

